1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,136
A colleague has read that there's a new
command that rewords an old commit in one

2
00:00:09,176 --> 00:00:12,080
line, with no todo list and
no editor full of pick.

3
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:16,640
A commit is one saved snapshot with a
message, and the todo list is the list of

4
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:20,103
instructions an interactive
rebase works through. It's true.

5
00:00:20,143 --> 00:00:24,606
The command exists in the Git on your
machine, and it does what the post says.

6
00:00:24,946 --> 00:00:29,344
It also moves every local branch that
contains the commit, without asking.

7
00:00:29,384 --> 00:00:33,603
It runs no hooks, the scripts Git can
run at fixed points of a command.

8
00:00:33,643 --> 00:00:38,461
It refuses any history with a merge in it,
and any operation that would conflict.

9
00:00:38,501 --> 00:00:43,019
And the first line of its manual page
is one word in capitals: EXPERIMENTAL.

10
00:00:43,059 --> 00:00:46,138
A guideline is a promise
about what works next year.

11
00:00:46,178 --> 00:00:49,616
This is not yet that. Hold on
to the colleague's question.

12
00:00:49,656 --> 00:00:52,855
By the end, a team lead
answers it in three sentences.

13
00:00:54,695 --> 00:00:58,359
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:58,399 --> 00:01:03,460
This video covers four new commands. Two of
them rewrite or replay commits without a

15
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:07,953
working tree, the directory of files
you edit: git history and git replay.

16
00:01:07,993 --> 00:01:11,900
Two of them inspect a repository:
git last-modified and git repo.

17
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:14,936
All four are marked
experimental in their manuals.

18
00:01:15,276 --> 00:01:19,600
In video 55 you learned interactive
rebase: reword, fixup, squash, edit.

19
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:24,630
You know what it does to the working tree,
to HEAD and to the state directory while

20
00:01:24,670 --> 00:01:29,599
it runs. That knowledge is the measuring
stick for today, because the question for

21
00:01:29,639 --> 00:01:34,690
each new command is the same: what does it
do differently, and what follows from the

22
00:01:34,730 --> 00:01:35,356
difference?

23
00:01:35,696 --> 00:01:42,342
Versions matter here, so listen for them.
Every "added in Git 2.56" in this video

24
00:01:42,382 --> 00:01:45,973
means: not run here.
The lab has Git 2.55.0.

25
00:01:47,813 --> 00:01:49,391
Here's what you'll walk away with.

26
00:01:49,731 --> 00:01:51,144
After this video you can:

27
00:01:51,484 --> 00:01:56,013
Reword, fix up and split a commit with
git history and say which branches move.

28
00:01:56,353 --> 00:02:01,901
Compare git history reword with an interactive
rebase: working tree, hooks, merges,

29
00:02:01,941 --> 00:02:02,574
conflicts.

30
00:02:02,914 --> 00:02:08,528
Explain why a server needs git replay
and what --ref-action=print gives it.

31
00:02:08,868 --> 00:02:14,400
Use git last-modified, git repo info and
git repo structure to inspect a repository.

32
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:19,880
State which of these commands are
experimental and which part is a Git 2.56

33
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:20,501
addition.

34
00:02:22,341 --> 00:02:27,029
git history, in one sentence. git history
rewrites one commit, its message, its

35
00:02:27,069 --> 00:02:32,057
content, or its division into two, and
replays every descendant on top, updating all

36
00:02:32,097 --> 00:02:37,024
local branches that contain the commit,
without touching a working tree and without

37
00:02:37,064 --> 00:02:39,179
a todo list. Its label is 🟡 CAUTION.

38
00:02:39,519 --> 00:02:44,801
Precisely. The command arrived in
Git 2.54 with reword and split.

39
00:02:44,841 --> 00:02:50,206
Git 2.55 added fixup. drop is new
in Git 2.56 and is not run here.

40
00:02:50,246 --> 00:02:54,054
The first line of its
manual says EXPERIMENTAL.

41
00:02:54,394 --> 00:02:58,774
Compared with git rebase -i, the
manual names three differences.

42
00:02:58,814 --> 00:03:04,021
Most subcommands need neither index nor
working tree, so they work in a bare

43
00:03:04,061 --> 00:03:08,233
repository, one with no working
tree, such as a server holds.

44
00:03:08,273 --> 00:03:13,619
No hooks are run. And by default every
branch that descends from the commit is

45
00:03:13,659 --> 00:03:18,453
updated, where a rebase moves one
branch unless you add --update-refs.

46
00:03:18,793 --> 00:03:23,152
Two limits are by design for now: no
merges in the affected history, and no

47
00:03:23,192 --> 00:03:24,911
operation that would conflict.

48
00:03:25,251 --> 00:03:29,787
Inside .git. New commit objects for
the target and all its descendants.

49
00:03:29,827 --> 00:03:34,815
Every affected branch ref moves, each
with a reflog entry of the form "reword:

50
00:03:34,855 --> 00:03:39,133
updating" followed by what you
typed. HEAD's reflog gets one entry.

51
00:03:39,473 --> 00:03:43,725
Read the "Remote" cell of the first row:
branches that were pushed now diverge.

52
00:03:43,765 --> 00:03:47,093
That is the sentence that decides
when not to use the command.

53
00:03:47,433 --> 00:03:52,713
The second row, fixup: the staged change
is consumed, and the rest is as above.

54
00:03:53,053 --> 00:03:58,296
The third, --dry-run: new objects
only, and everything else unchanged.

55
00:03:58,636 --> 00:04:04,017
When to use it, and when not. The default
of moving every descendant branch is right

56
00:04:04,057 --> 00:04:08,921
for a stack of local branches and wrong
the moment one of them is published.

57
00:04:08,961 --> 00:04:14,020
Because no hooks run, a message convention
enforced by a commit-msg hook is not

58
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:19,312
checked. So: use it for local cleanup before
the first push, read --dry-run first,

59
00:04:19,352 --> 00:04:23,377
and keep git rebase -i for
everything with merges or conflicts.

60
00:04:23,717 --> 00:04:28,920
One observation from the lab, which the
textbook marks as unverified because the

61
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:34,098
manual does not state it: on Git 2.55.0,
git history reword gives the rewritten

62
00:04:34,138 --> 00:04:39,603
commit a new committer date, although the
manual says that all other details stay as

63
00:04:39,643 --> 00:04:40,258
they were.

64
00:04:40,598 --> 00:04:46,238
git replay. From section 9.20: git replay
replays a range onto a new base without

65
00:04:46,278 --> 00:04:51,708
touching the working tree or the index,
so it also works in a bare repository.

66
00:04:51,748 --> 00:04:57,459
The command exists for servers. A hosting
service that rebases or cherry-picks for

67
00:04:57,499 --> 00:05:03,279
you has no working tree to do it in. It was
introduced in Git 2.44 as a server-side

68
00:05:03,319 --> 00:05:03,630
tool.

69
00:05:03,970 --> 00:05:07,948
Three forms. --onto rebases
a range onto a new base.

70
00:05:08,288 --> 00:05:13,472
--advance=<branch> is a cherry-pick of the
range onto that branch, and the original

71
00:05:13,512 --> 00:05:18,696
author is kept. Here is the demo's repository
before it: main, and fix/timeout with

72
00:05:18,736 --> 00:05:19,452
two commits.

73
00:05:19,792 --> 00:05:24,646
After the advance, main carries copies of
the two commits, and the originals stay on

74
00:05:24,686 --> 00:05:25,345
fix/timeout.

75
00:05:25,685 --> 00:05:30,877
And since Git 2.54 the same
machinery reverts, with --revert.

76
00:05:30,917 --> 00:05:35,508
--linearize was added in Git
2.56 and is not run here.

77
00:05:35,848 --> 00:05:42,031
--ref-action=print creates the commits and
prints the ref update, which a server can

78
00:05:42,071 --> 00:05:48,107
inspect or feed to git update-ref --stdin.
Without it, the default since Git 2.53,

79
00:05:48,147 --> 00:05:50,552
the ref moves in one transaction.

80
00:05:50,892 --> 00:05:54,766
The price of having no working tree:
it cannot stop for a conflict.

81
00:05:54,806 --> 00:05:59,440
A replay that would conflict exits with
status 1, prints nothing, and leaves the

82
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:00,608
branch where it was.

83
00:06:00,948 --> 00:06:06,516
And a second price in a bare repository:
a bare repository keeps no reflog, the

84
00:06:06,556 --> 00:06:11,840
local record of where a ref has been,
unless core.logAllRefUpdates says so.

85
00:06:11,880 --> 00:06:14,396
So a replay there has no local undo.

86
00:06:14,736 --> 00:06:19,216
Its value for you is mostly indirect: it
is the kind of operation behind a "rebase

87
00:06:19,256 --> 00:06:20,208
and merge" button.

88
00:06:20,548 --> 00:06:25,704
git last-modified and git repo. Two
experimental commands from Git 2.52 answer

89
00:06:25,744 --> 00:06:29,367
questions that used to need
a loop or a peek into .git.

90
00:06:29,707 --> 00:06:34,556
git last-modified prints, for each path,
the commit that last changed it: the "last

91
00:06:34,596 --> 00:06:38,266
commit" column of a file browser.
With -r it descends to files.

92
00:06:38,606 --> 00:06:44,492
git repo info returns named facts about
a repository in a stable key=value form,

93
00:06:44,532 --> 00:06:49,380
which is what a script should use
in place of reading .git/config.

94
00:06:49,420 --> 00:06:55,601
Git 2.56 adds path.* keys, not run here.
git repo structure counts refs and objects.

95
00:06:55,941 --> 00:07:00,491
The rule for all four. Don't adopt
experimental commands in shared automation.

96
00:07:00,531 --> 00:07:05,256
Output and options may change between
releases. Use them at the keyboard, and pin

97
00:07:05,296 --> 00:07:07,963
the Git version where a
script depends on one.

98
00:07:09,803 --> 00:07:14,521
A picture helps. Think of two ways to
correct a typo on page one of a bound

99
00:07:14,561 --> 00:07:15,219
manuscript.

100
00:07:15,559 --> 00:07:18,787
Interactive rebase takes the
binding apart on your desk.

101
00:07:18,827 --> 00:07:23,573
Page by page it rebuilds the book in front
of you, and while it works your desk is

102
00:07:23,613 --> 00:07:28,418
covered: the working tree is checked out
at each step, HEAD is detached, hooks run,

103
00:07:28,458 --> 00:07:31,744
and if a page doesn't fit
you're asked to fix it by hand.

104
00:07:32,084 --> 00:07:37,353
git history works in the archive. It writes
a corrected page one and fresh copies of

105
00:07:37,393 --> 00:07:42,471
every later page, in the object database,
and then moves the shelf labels, all of

106
00:07:42,511 --> 00:07:45,378
them, in one step. Your
desk is never touched.

107
00:07:45,718 --> 00:07:50,254
That's why it's fast, why it works in a
bare repository, why no hook is called, and

108
00:07:50,294 --> 00:07:54,885
why it can't ask you to resolve a conflict:
there is no desk to put the conflict on.

109
00:07:55,225 --> 00:08:00,497
Quick quiz. You reword a commit with git
history. Which shelf labels move: A, only

110
00:08:00,537 --> 00:08:05,615
the branch you're on, B, every local
branch that contains the commit, or C, the

111
00:08:05,655 --> 00:08:08,142
server's branches as well? Your answer?

112
00:08:12,982 --> 00:08:17,537
B. Every local branch that contains the
commit. The server's branches don't move,

113
00:08:17,577 --> 00:08:20,430
which is why branches that
were pushed now diverge.

114
00:08:20,770 --> 00:08:25,823
Where the picture breaks: an archivist would
ask before moving a label that somebody

115
00:08:25,863 --> 00:08:30,794
else's catalogue points at. git history
moves every local branch that contains the

116
00:08:30,834 --> 00:08:34,856
commit and asks nothing. The reflog
of each branch has the old tip.

117
00:08:34,896 --> 00:08:36,311
There is no single undo.

118
00:08:38,151 --> 00:08:42,853
Build the left column from what you know
from video 55, then the right column line

119
00:08:42,893 --> 00:08:47,537
by line against it. The bottom drawing is
the difference to remember: on the left

120
00:08:47,577 --> 00:08:52,337
topic keeps pointing at the old commits
unless you ask, and on the right it's moved

121
00:08:52,377 --> 00:08:52,800
for you.

122
00:08:54,639 --> 00:08:56,537
What the command offers on this version.

123
00:08:56,877 --> 00:09:01,285
Three subcommands on Git
2.55: fixup, reword, split.

124
00:09:01,325 --> 00:09:08,469
No drop: that is the 2.56 addition. Each
takes --dry-run, and --update-refs with two

125
00:09:08,509 --> 00:09:10,693
values, branches and head.

126
00:09:11,033 --> 00:09:16,769
Try it now, in any repository of your own.
Thirty seconds, and it only prints help:

127
00:09:16,809 --> 00:09:22,268
git history -h. It needs Git 2.54 or
newer, and an older Git reports an unknown

128
00:09:22,308 --> 00:09:26,722
command. Predict first: how many
subcommands will your Git list?

129
00:09:31,562 --> 00:09:36,923
Three means your Git matches the lab. A
fourth, drop, means you have 2.56 or newer.

130
00:09:36,963 --> 00:09:41,413
And if Git doesn't know the command
at all, yours is older than 2.54.

131
00:09:41,453 --> 00:09:45,773
Whichever you saw, you've read your
own Git instead of a blog post.

132
00:09:46,113 --> 00:09:51,754
Reword. The repository has a typo in its
oldest commit and three branches above it.

133
00:09:51,794 --> 00:09:57,367
🟡 CAUTION: git history reword creates new
commits and moves every descendant local

134
00:09:57,407 --> 00:10:01,406
branch. Preview: --dry-run.
Recovery: each branch's reflog.

135
00:10:01,446 --> 00:10:07,019
Predict: after rewording the oldest commit,
how many commit IDs in this graph stay

136
00:10:07,059 --> 00:10:08,798
the same? Say it out loud.

137
00:10:13,638 --> 00:10:19,139
None. The editor opened with the old message,
and the message was replaced: "metirc"

138
00:10:19,179 --> 00:10:24,349
became "metric". Every commit ID changed,
including 67d2d0a on the side branch,

139
00:10:24,389 --> 00:10:25,734
which is now 131e7a7.

140
00:10:26,074 --> 00:10:32,576
As a picture. Before: five commits, with
main, release/0.1 and topic/judge on them.

141
00:10:32,916 --> 00:10:37,022
After: five new commits, and all
three labels moved onto them.

142
00:10:37,062 --> 00:10:42,506
The old five are dashed. No branch reaches
them any more, and each branch's reflog

143
00:10:42,546 --> 00:10:43,977
still has its old tip.

144
00:10:44,317 --> 00:10:50,125
main, release/0.1 and topic/judge all point
into the new history, each with its own

145
00:10:50,165 --> 00:10:55,481
reflog entry. And git status says we
are on main, as if nothing happened: no

146
00:10:55,521 --> 00:10:56,890
checkout took place.

147
00:10:57,230 --> 00:10:57,917
Dry run.

148
00:10:58,257 --> 00:11:03,362
A dry run writes the new objects and prints
the ref updates instead of making them,

149
00:11:03,402 --> 00:11:07,888
in the input format of git update-ref
--stdin: three branches would move.

150
00:11:07,928 --> 00:11:13,033
The log afterwards is unchanged. This is
the list to read before every real run: is

151
00:11:13,073 --> 00:11:15,017
any of these branches published?

152
00:11:15,357 --> 00:11:20,493
Fixup. A staged change is folded into an
older commit, and its descendants are

153
00:11:20,533 --> 00:11:24,873
replayed. This is the autosquash
workflow of video 56 in one step.

154
00:11:25,213 --> 00:11:28,317
The status is empty: the
staged change was consumed.

155
00:11:28,357 --> 00:11:31,643
The commit two back now
contains the test file as well.

156
00:11:31,983 --> 00:11:37,101
Split. split asks, hunk by hunk, what to
move into a new commit that becomes the

157
00:11:37,141 --> 00:11:42,517
parent of the original. The demo answers
yes to the first hunk and no to the second,

158
00:11:42,557 --> 00:11:44,193
and supplies two messages.

159
00:11:52,533 --> 00:11:56,937
One commit became two: "Add F1 metric" with
the function, then "Test the F1 metric"

160
00:11:56,977 --> 00:12:00,256
with the test, and the two later
commits were replayed on top.

161
00:12:00,596 --> 00:12:05,164
No hooks. A commit-msg hook that
refuses every message is installed.

162
00:12:05,204 --> 00:12:08,416
Predict: will it stop a
reword? Say it out loud.

163
00:12:13,256 --> 00:12:18,259
An ordinary git commit is refused by the
hook. git history reword goes through, and

164
00:12:18,299 --> 00:12:22,816
the new message is in the log. If your
team's message convention lives in a

165
00:12:22,856 --> 00:12:25,490
commit-msg hook, this
command walks past it.

166
00:12:25,830 --> 00:12:27,992
The limits, each with its real message.

167
00:12:28,332 --> 00:12:33,622
A fixup that would conflict: "fixup would
produce conflicts; aborting", exit status

168
00:12:33,662 --> 00:12:37,475
255, and the staged change is
still staged. Nothing changed.

169
00:12:37,515 --> 00:12:42,741
A history with a merge above the target:
"replaying merge commits is not supported

170
00:12:42,781 --> 00:12:46,016
yet!". Both are refusals,
not half-finished states.

171
00:12:46,056 --> 00:12:48,585
That is the good side of having no desk.

172
00:12:48,925 --> 00:12:49,952
A bare repository.

173
00:12:50,292 --> 00:12:55,215
No working tree. fix/timeout has two commits
and branches off an old commit of main.

174
00:12:55,555 --> 00:13:01,872
Print the update. 🟢 for this form: with
--ref-action=print only new objects are

175
00:13:01,912 --> 00:13:02,516
written.

176
00:13:02,856 --> 00:13:07,314
One line in update-ref format: the
ref, the new value, the old value.

177
00:13:07,354 --> 00:13:12,659
main itself has not moved. A server can
inspect that line, check rules against it,

178
00:13:12,699 --> 00:13:13,832
and then apply it.

179
00:13:14,172 --> 00:13:19,448
Advance. 🟡 CAUTION: git replay --advance
creates commits and moves the ref in one

180
00:13:19,488 --> 00:13:24,501
transaction. Preview: the print form you
saw. Recovery: the reflog, where one

181
00:13:24,541 --> 00:13:28,044
exists. There is none by
default in a bare repository.

182
00:13:28,384 --> 00:13:34,140
main now carries copies of the two commits,
dc10709 and 863b8b5, and the originals

183
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:39,724
are still on fix/timeout. It's a cherry-pick
of a range, done where no checkout

184
00:13:39,764 --> 00:13:45,449
exists. The printed update named 1653f1a,
not 863b8b5: the two runs made the same

185
00:13:45,489 --> 00:13:49,548
commits at different committer
times, so their IDs differ.

186
00:13:49,888 --> 00:13:51,672
Revert, and the missing reflog.

187
00:13:52,012 --> 00:13:57,433
The revert commit has the usual message.
And the last command printed nothing: this

188
00:13:57,473 --> 00:14:01,710
bare repository keeps no reflog,
so the replay has no local undo.

189
00:14:02,050 --> 00:14:06,283
The conflict case. Replay
labs/run ch09/replay-conflict.

190
00:14:06,323 --> 00:14:10,786
Predict what a replay prints
when the range would conflict.

191
00:14:10,826 --> 00:14:12,007
Say it out loud.

192
00:14:16,847 --> 00:14:20,512
Exit status 1, no output, and
the branch is where it was.

193
00:14:20,552 --> 00:14:23,892
No message, no state directory,
nothing to continue.

194
00:14:23,932 --> 00:14:29,027
In a script, the exit status is the only
signal. For a conflict you rebase in a

195
00:14:29,067 --> 00:14:29,871
working tree.

196
00:14:30,211 --> 00:14:31,326
git last-modified.

197
00:14:31,666 --> 00:14:33,914
One line per entry of the top-level tree.

198
00:14:34,254 --> 00:14:39,619
For the directory src the answer is the
merge commit, 5bcf6ca, because the two sides

199
00:14:39,659 --> 00:14:44,445
changed different files in it and only
the merge produced the present tree.

200
00:14:44,485 --> 00:14:48,885
With -r it descends to files, and
each file names an ordinary commit.

201
00:14:49,225 --> 00:14:55,210
The same answer for one path, the way it
was asked before Git 2.52: git log -1 with

202
00:14:55,250 --> 00:14:58,622
a path. And a revision
range limits the search.

203
00:14:58,962 --> 00:15:01,205
git repo info and git repo structure.

204
00:15:01,545 --> 00:15:06,564
Four keys on this version: whether the
repository is bare, whether it is shallow,

205
00:15:06,604 --> 00:15:11,684
the object format and the ref format. This
is what a script should ask in place of

206
00:15:11,724 --> 00:15:16,618
reading .git/config, and it's what you
would have used in the release script of

207
00:15:16,658 --> 00:15:17,243
video 199.

208
00:15:17,583 --> 00:15:22,760
Counts of refs and objects by kind, and
their inflated sizes, as lines a script can

209
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:23,137
parse.

210
00:15:24,977 --> 00:15:26,625
Five mistakes to watch for.

211
00:15:26,965 --> 00:15:30,264
Running git history on a commit
that was already pushed.

212
00:15:30,304 --> 00:15:35,213
Root cause: it moves every local branch
that contains the commit, so each published

213
00:15:35,253 --> 00:15:37,360
branch now diverges from the server.

214
00:15:37,700 --> 00:15:42,838
Expecting hooks to check a reworded message.
Root cause: the command runs no hooks,

215
00:15:42,878 --> 00:15:46,206
so a convention enforced by
commit-msg is not applied.

216
00:15:46,546 --> 00:15:51,927
Reading exit status 1 from git replay as
a crash. Root cause: with no working tree

217
00:15:51,967 --> 00:15:57,282
it cannot stop for a conflict, so it reports
one by exiting with 1 and no output.

218
00:15:57,622 --> 00:16:00,863
Putting an experimental command
into shared automation.

219
00:16:00,903 --> 00:16:05,755
Root cause: output and options may change
between releases, and the manual says so

220
00:16:05,795 --> 00:16:06,829
in its first line.

221
00:16:07,169 --> 00:16:11,193
Assuming a replay in a bare
repository can be undone locally.

222
00:16:11,233 --> 00:16:16,056
Root cause: a bare repository keeps
no reflog unless it is configured to.

223
00:16:17,896 --> 00:16:22,992
Now, out of the lab. A team lead is asked
the hook's question: do we change our

224
00:16:23,032 --> 00:16:25,658
guidelines? She tests before she answers.

225
00:16:25,998 --> 00:16:31,405
In a sandbox she takes a typical local
stack: three small branches for one feature,

226
00:16:31,445 --> 00:16:36,589
none of them pushed. A typo in the first
commit's message is fixed with one git

227
00:16:36,629 --> 00:16:41,118
history reword, after a dry run that
lists three branches, all local.

228
00:16:41,158 --> 00:16:45,777
All three move together. She notes
that this used to need a rebase with

229
00:16:45,817 --> 00:16:46,696
--update-refs.

230
00:16:47,036 --> 00:16:51,732
Then she tries what the team's guidelines
protect. The message convention is

231
00:16:51,772 --> 00:16:55,160
enforced by a commit-msg hook:
the reword goes past it.

232
00:16:55,200 --> 00:17:00,332
A second stack has a merge in it: refused.
A branch that is already pushed: the dry

233
00:17:00,372 --> 00:17:02,451
run lists it, and she stops there.

234
00:17:02,791 --> 00:17:07,685
Her answer to the team is three sentences,
and it's the answer to the opening.

235
00:17:07,725 --> 00:17:12,873
The guidelines don't change: interactive
rebase stays the documented tool, because

236
00:17:12,913 --> 00:17:17,934
it runs hooks and handles merges and
conflicts. git history is allowed for local

237
00:17:17,974 --> 00:17:21,350
cleanup before the first
push, always after --dry-run.

238
00:17:21,390 --> 00:17:24,830
And nothing experimental goes
into the release scripts.

239
00:17:24,870 --> 00:17:30,144
Where a script wants git repo info, the
script pins the Git version and says so in a

240
00:17:30,184 --> 00:17:30,650
comment.

241
00:17:32,490 --> 00:17:39,855
Your turn. Do Lab 42.2, "git history
reword", in lab-manual/m42-frontier.md.

242
00:17:40,195 --> 00:17:43,950
Before the dry run, predict which
branches it will list and why.

243
00:17:43,990 --> 00:17:48,634
Before the real reword, predict what git
status -sb will print afterwards for a

244
00:17:48,674 --> 00:17:53,200
branch that has an upstream. In the lab's
failure scenario a pushed commit is

245
00:17:53,240 --> 00:17:58,181
reworded: before you repair it, write down
where the old tip of each affected branch

246
00:17:58,221 --> 00:18:01,561
can be found and how many
separate repairs you will need.

247
00:18:01,901 --> 00:18:06,840
The challenge: in a sandbox, rebuild Lab
9.1 with git history where it applies, and

248
00:18:06,880 --> 00:18:09,839
note which steps still need
an interactive rebase.

249
00:18:11,679 --> 00:18:13,732
Read the question, then answer out loud.

250
00:18:18,572 --> 00:18:23,190
The question hands you five headings. Use
them as your structure and give each one

251
00:18:23,230 --> 00:18:27,848
sentence for each command. A strong answer
explains the differences from one cause

252
00:18:27,888 --> 00:18:32,223
instead of listing five facts: what it
means that one of the two never checks

253
00:18:32,263 --> 00:18:32,961
anything out.

254
00:18:33,301 --> 00:18:36,255
It states the version and the
status of the newer command.

255
00:18:36,295 --> 00:18:40,436
And it ends, as the rule says, on a limit
and a control: when you would refuse to

256
00:18:40,476 --> 00:18:44,669
use the newer command, and what you would
run first to find out whether you are in

257
00:18:44,709 --> 00:18:45,185
that case.

258
00:18:47,025 --> 00:18:47,952
Let's land this.

259
00:18:48,292 --> 00:18:53,215
git history rewords, fixes up or splits
one commit without a working tree, replays

260
00:18:53,255 --> 00:18:57,573
every descendant, and moves every
local branch that contains the commit.

261
00:18:57,913 --> 00:19:04,679
It runs no hooks and refuses merges and
conflicts; it is experimental, with reword

262
00:19:04,719 --> 00:19:11,402
and split since Git 2.54, fixup since 2.55,
and drop added in 2.56, not run here.

263
00:19:11,742 --> 00:19:17,236
git replay rebases, cherry-picks or reverts
without a working tree, for servers;

264
00:19:17,276 --> 00:19:22,839
--ref-action=print prints the ref update
instead of making it; a conflict is exit

265
00:19:22,879 --> 00:19:24,499
status 1 with no output.

266
00:19:24,839 --> 00:19:29,556
git last-modified names the commit that
last changed each path; git repo info and

267
00:19:29,596 --> 00:19:33,667
git repo structure report facts and
counts in a form scripts can read.

268
00:19:34,007 --> 00:19:39,213
Use all four at the keyboard, read --dry-run
or the printed update first, and keep

269
00:19:39,253 --> 00:19:42,220
experimental commands out
of shared automation.

270
00:19:44,060 --> 00:19:49,415
Read sections 14D.6 to 14D.8 and section
9.20. Then open the manual page of git

271
00:19:49,455 --> 00:19:54,399
history in your own installation and
check its first line and its list of

272
00:19:54,439 --> 00:19:59,998
subcommands against this video. If they
differ, your Git is newer than the course,

273
00:20:00,038 --> 00:20:01,637
and the manual is right.

274
00:20:01,977 --> 00:20:06,751
Today you measured four experimental commands
against a tool you already know, and

275
00:20:06,791 --> 00:20:11,683
you can say where each is safe to use. Next
time, the last video of the course: Rust

276
00:20:11,723 --> 00:20:16,438
in Git, stacked workflows, release notes,
and the patch-based workflow of the Git

277
00:20:16,478 --> 00:20:20,020
